<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venetian Arsenal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1104</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Arsenale di Venezia, Arsenale Nuovo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Venetian Arsenal is a complex of former state shipyards and armouries in the Castello district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> that built most of the Venetian Republic's naval power from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> to the early modern period. Usually said to have been begun in 1104, it was greatly enlarged with the Arsenale Nuovo of 1320 and became the largest industrial complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, covering about 45 hectares, some 15 percent of the city. At its peak in the early 16th century some 16,000 workers built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> from standardised parts at a rate of nearly one a day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> advised it from 1593. It served the Republic until its fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1797 and is now a naval base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venetian-arsenal.jpg" alt="Venetian Arsenal" width="330" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campo_de_l'Arsenal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
